title: Collapsing a revert to one operation per cell
tags: [[Revert]]
kind: question
audience: dev
status: deferred
summary: A revert could write one operation per cell instead of one per reverted operation; deferred — it would be a second way of computing a revert.

A revert writes one operation per reverted operation. It could write one per ''cell'':
for each cell the set touches, the state before the set's earliest operation on it,
written once — reverting a four-operation retype would take one operation.

Deferred deliberately: the gain is log volume, on revisions rarely large; the cost is a
second way of computing what a revert writes, with its own case analysis for the
entity-wide and `file_*` types, and a correctness that no longer follows from the
rollback inverses the tests already cover. Worth revisiting only if batch reverts turn
out to bloat the log in practice.
